Subject: [PATCH] winex11: shaped + real-alpha transparency for layered 3D-overlay windows
 (ALPHA-mode readback throttle, tunable hysteresis, real per-pixel-alpha click-through)

Borderless WS_EX_LAYERED windows that paint per-pixel-alpha overlays through a 3D client
surface (DWM-glass style; e.g. desktop/taskbar overlay games) render transparent regions as
opaque black under Proton. Two opt-in modes (off by default):
 * WINE_LAYERED_OVERLAY_SHAPE=1 - XShape the window to rendered non-black pixels.
 * WINE_LAYERED_OVERLAY_ALPHA=1 - real per-pixel alpha via a 32-bit ARGB visual + a per-pixel
   ShapeInput region for click-through. Requires a compositor.

Improvements over the original (thaylorz/Solarisfire) patch:
 * ALPHA mode no longer does a full GPU->CPU XGetImage readback every present. That readback
   only feeds the click-through input shape, so it is throttled to WINE_LAYERED_OVERLAY_INPUT_HZ
   (default 60 Hz; 0 = every present). First frame and every resize still refresh immediately.
 * Shape hysteresis tunable via WINE_LAYERED_OVERLAY_HYSTERESIS (default 12).
 * ALPHA-mode click-through is derived from the real per-pixel ALPHA (32-bit ARGB), not
   luminance, so opaque-but-dark pixels (e.g. a sprite's black outline) stay clickable instead
   of becoming click-through holes. Falls back to luminance if the readback isn't 32-bit, with
   a safety net so a driver that returns no readable alpha can't make the overlay uninteractive.
 * Readback now uses MIT-SHM (XShmGetImage into a persistent shared-memory image, server
   writes pixels directly — no per-frame socket transfer of the whole frame), with a clean
   fallback to XGetImage when SHM is unavailable. Mirrors wine's bitblt.c shm idiom.
